=== layout: subsection

# But what if genes are on the [same chromosome]{color=red}?

=== layout: one-panel

# *Linked Genes*

@body

- Each chromosome has hundreds or thousands of genes (except the Y chromosome)
- Genes located on the same chromosome that tend to be inherited together are called [*linked genes*]{underline=true}

=== layout: one-panel

# *_Cis_ and _Trans_ Configurations*

@body

![Slide image 6](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/image_006.jpg)

=== layout: two-over-one-panels
@replaceme

# *Cis-Trans Isomers*

@top-left

## [*_cis_*]{color=red} [*isomer*]{color=red}*\: The two Xs are on the same side.*

![Cis isomer](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/cis_isomer.svg)

@top-right

## [*_trans_*]{color=red} [*isomer*]{color=red}*\: The two Xs are on opposite sides.*

![Trans isomer](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/trans_isomer.svg)

@bottom

- Geometric isomers are compounds with the same covalent partnerships that differ in their spatial arrangement around a carbon-carbon double bond.
- The double bond does not allow atoms to rotate freely around the bond axis.

