=== layout: section

# Recombination

=== layout: one-panel

# *How Linkage Affects Inheritance*

@body

- Morgan did other experiments with fruit flies to see how linkage affects inheritance of two characters
- Morgan crossed flies that differed in traits of body color and wing size

=== layout: one-panel

@body

![Labeled diagram from source slide 21](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/b21_labeled.svg)

- Morgan mated true-breeding P (parental) generation flies to produce heterozygous F1 dihybrids all of which are wild-type in appearance.
- He then mated wild-type F1 dihybrid females with homozygous recessive males.
- This testcross will reveal the genotype of the eggs made by the dihybrid female.

@notes
Source: lect08b-chap8-linked_genes.odp, slide 21.
One source artwork image and overlapping labels reconstructed as a quality-90 WebP embedded in SVG with live text. Titles and captions remain native.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Morgan found that body color and wing size are usually inherited together in specific combinations (parental phenotypes)
- He noted that these [*genes do not assort independently*]{underline=true}, and reasoned that they were on the same chromosome

